= 1 \\cdot r^{4 - 1} \\\\\n8 &= r^{3} \\\\\n2^{3} &= r^{3} \\\\\n2 &= r\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_{n} = 1 \\cdot 2^{n - 1}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives $a_n=32$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 1 \\cdot 2^{n - 1} \\\\\n32 &= 2^{n - 1} \\\\\n2^{5} &= 2^{n - 1} \\\\\n5 &= n - 1 \\\\\n6 &= n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $n=6$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078206 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1516" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078206 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that a geometric sequence has $a_2 = 24$, $a_5 = 3$ and $a_k = 12$, what is the value of $k$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078206 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e3fed3e8-9bc6-558d-a426-96006871a20d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078206 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the common ratio. We are told that $a_2=24$ and $a_5=3$, so we can find the common ratio using a formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{n - m} &= \\frac{a_{n}}{a_{m}} \\\\\nr^{5 - 2} &= \\frac{a_{5}}{a_{2}} \\\\\nr^{5 - 2} &= \\frac{3}{24} \\\\\nr^{3} &= \\frac{1}{8} \\\\\nr^{3} &= (\\frac{1}{2})^{3} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n, we can use the same formula to solve for the value of $k$ such that $a_k=12$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{n - m} &= \\frac{a_{n}}{a_{m}} \\\\\nr^{k - 2} &= \\frac{a_{k}}{a_{2}} \\\\\n(\\frac{1}{2})^{k - 2} &= \\frac{12}{24} \\\\\n(\\frac{1}{2})^{k - 2} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n(\\frac{1}{2})^{k - 2} &= (\\frac{1}{2})^{1} \\\\\nk - 2 &= 1 \\\\\nk &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078306 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078306 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the index of the term $16$ for the geometric sequence below?\n\n$a_{n+1} = 2 a_n \\quad a_1 = \\dfrac{1}{4},\\quad n\\geq 1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078306 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d7c0ca60-1915-5484-913a-835a1f8fa320" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078306 #edb/ref 1074 "This geometric sequence is given as a recurrence relation, i.e.,\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = {\\color{blue}r} \\cdot a_n\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na_1 = \\dfrac{1}{4}, \\quad {\\color{blue}r}=2\n$$\n\nWe're interested in finding $n$ such that $a_n = 16$. Using the formula for the $n$th term, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n16 &= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 2^{n - 1} \\\\\n2^{4} &= \\frac{1}{2^{2}} \\cdot 2^{n - 1} \\\\\n2^{6} &= 2^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents gives:\n\n$$\n6 = n - 1\\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad n = 7\n$$\n\nTherefore, $16$ is the $7$th term of the sequence." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078307 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4241" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078307 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that a geometric sequence has the first term $a_1 = 5$, the common ratio $r = 2$, and the $n$th term $a_n = 640$, what is the value of $n$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078307 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2da03f9b-c474-5b07-b4e8-bdf1dc3c2fd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078307 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the index, we substitute our known values of $a_1$, $r$ and $a_n$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n640 &= 5 \\cdot 2^{n - 1} \\\\\n\\frac{640}{5} &= 2^{n - 1} \\\\\n128 &= 2^{n - 1} \\\\\n2^{7} &= 2^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents gives:\n\n$$\n7 = n - 1\\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad n = 8\n$$\n\nWe can check this result by substituting $n = 8$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 2^{8 - 1} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 2^{7} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 128 \\\\\n&= 640✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078436 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078436 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078436 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078436 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078436 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Index of a Term Given the Recursive Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078436 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078436 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "90216c0e-e4a5-5933-86e0-58d218939e29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078437 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078437 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078437 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078437 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Index of a Term Given the First Term and the Common Ratio" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078437 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078437 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a82355f8-4b86-55c8-8686-1e4ea82e4464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078564 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078564 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078564 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Index of a Term Given the First Term and Another Term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078564 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078564 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d47c0304-a438-5444-9add-5e4f61acbbcd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078565 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078565 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078565 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078565 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Index of a Particular Term When the First Term is Unknown" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078565 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078565 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "aaa87d67-d9df-5bbb-b98e-00d72d16fe7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078698 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078698 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078698 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078698 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078698 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078698 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078698 #edb/ref 1045 "Determining Indexes of Terms in Geometric Sequences" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078698 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186079509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078698 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078698 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "8560c2d0-d837-56e0-950c-db576aea6153" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079114 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186077462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079114 #edb/ref 1069 "q-5418" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079114 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that a positive geometric sequence has\n$a_{3} = 12$, $a_{5} = 3$ and $a_{k} = 48$, what is the value of $k$?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079114 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cf5edc3d-d94c-5428-950a-0bf9dfc9cd27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079114 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079155 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186077708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079155 #edb/ref 1069 "q-63920" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079155 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that a geometric sequence has the first term $a_{1} = 3$, the common ratio $r = 2$, and the $n$th term $a_{n} = 24$, what is the value of $n$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079155 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56cba562-9371-5b87-a542-8607369c16b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079313 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079313 #edb/ref 1061 15490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079313 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a9588d2a-14e4-52e1-b197-e06616dcb6e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079313 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079314 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079314 #edb/ref 1061 15491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079314 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "23a88b1d-eef3-5987-a6c3-5e388244d80e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079314 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079509 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079509 #edb/ref 1061 4734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079509 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fbfa3bf3-20c5-5d4d-a7dd-74222bd4805e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079509 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079510 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079510 #edb/ref 1061 4735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079510 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a4bf9b19-3f3a-5399-8f55-c85f6684f07d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079510 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079511 #edb/ref 1061 4741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079511 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a73a35bf-c363-53aa-a8e5-abf04854ae43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079511 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079627 #edb/ref 1064 "In addition to using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto compute the terms of a geometric sequence, we can also use it to compute the index of a desired term in a geometric sequence.\n\nTo illustrate, suppose we're given the geometric sequence\n\n$$\n3, \\: 6, \\: 12, \\: \\ldots, \\:96, \\ldots\n$$\n\nthat has the first term $a_1=3$ and the common ratio $r=2$. What index gives the term $96$? In other words, what value of $n$ gives $a_n=96$?\n\nWe can construct the formula for the $n$th term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 2^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the value of $n$ that gives $a_n=96$, we can use the formula for the $n$th term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 3 \\cdot 2^{n - 1} \\\\\n96 &= 3 \\cdot 2^{n - 1} \\\\\n32 &= 2^{n - 1} \\\\\n2^{5} &= 2^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents gives:\n\n$$\n5 = n - 1\\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad n = 6\n$$\n\nTherefore, $96$ is the $6$th term of the sequence." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079627 #edb/ref 1065 1672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079627 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "77450737-c02f-5751-b70b-0b387def70eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079627 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
